---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI EAS integration" activeNav="/cli/integrations/eas">
  <h1>EAS</h1>
  <p>
    <code>appmctl eas</code> covers the Expo integration. The API splits it across two scopes,
    and so does the command: the <strong>Expo account</strong> hangs off an integration, and the
    <strong>project link</strong> hangs off an app.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two actions exist in both scopes. Rather than inventing separate names, they take the scope
    from the flag you pass — <code>--integration-id</code> for the account,
    <code>--app</code> for the project link — and refuse to run when handed both:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl eas sync --integration-id int-002   # re-sync the account and its project list
appmctl eas sync --app customer-app        # re-sync one linked project

appmctl eas sync --app customer-app --integration-id int-002
# appmctl: pass either --app or --integration-id, not both`} />

  <h2>Account actions</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl eas connect  --token TOKEN [--account NAME]
appmctl eas projects --integration-id ID
appmctl eas import   --integration-id ID --project-id UUID
appmctl eas sync       --integration-id ID
appmctl eas disconnect --integration-id ID`} />

  <h3>Connect an account</h3>
  <p>
    An Expo access token is all that is needed. Pass the token through a variable so it does not
    land in shell history, and <code>--account</code> only when the token can see more than one
    account:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl eas connect --token "$EXPO_TOKEN" --account acme

# id:          int-002
# provider:    expo
# account:     acme
# status:      active
# created_at:  2026-08-03T10:15:00+00:00`} />
  <p>The token is withheld from dry-run output, the same as credential payloads:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --dry-run eas connect --token "$EXPO_TOKEN"

# dry-run: no request sent
# method: POST
# url: https://manager.example.com/api/v1/acme-mobile/integrations/expo
# body: expo credentials (58 bytes; redacted)`} />

  <h3>List and import projects</h3>
  <p>
    <code>projects</code> lists what the account can see, with <code>on_expo</code> showing
    whether the project still exists upstream. <code>import</code> creates an App Manager app
    from one of them:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl eas projects --integration-id int-002

# id              project                  expo_id                               on_expo  last_synced
# --------------  -----------------------  ------------------------------------  -------  -----------
# expo-proj-001   @acme/customer-app       11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555  true     2026-08-02T18:20:00+00:00
# expo-proj-002   @acme/internal-tools     66666666-7777-8888-9999-000000000000  false

appmctl eas import --integration-id int-002 --project-id expo-proj-001`} />

  <h3>Sync and disconnect</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl eas sync --integration-id int-002
appmctl eas disconnect --integration-id int-002
# Expo account disconnected from integration int-002`} />
  <p>
    Disconnecting the account removes the stored token and leaves every linked project without a
    working connection. To unlink one app instead, use <code>disconnect --app</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Project actions</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl eas status            [--app APP]
appmctl eas link              [--app APP] --integration-id ID --mode create|existing [--project-id UUID]
appmctl eas auto-sync         [--app APP] --enabled true|false
appmctl eas identifiers       [--app APP]
appmctl eas sync-identifiers  [--app APP]
appmctl eas provision         [--app APP] --identifier-id ID [--credential-ids ID,ID]
appmctl eas attach-credential [--app APP] --identifier-id ID --credential-id ID
appmctl eas sync       --app APP
appmctl eas disconnect --app APP`} />

  <h3>Link an app to a project</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--mode create</code> makes a new Expo project for the app.
    <code>--mode existing</code> links to one that already exists and requires
    <code>--project-id</code>, which the CLI checks before sending:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# New Expo project
appmctl eas link --integration-id int-002 --mode create

# Existing project
appmctl eas link --integration-id int-002 --mode existing \\
  --project-id 11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555

appmctl eas link --integration-id int-002 --mode existing
# appmctl: --project-id is required when --mode is existing`} />

  <h3>Status</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl eas status

# project:           @acme/customer-app
# slug:              customer-app
# account:           acme
# expo_project_id:   11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555
# exists_on_expo:    true
# automatic_sync:    true
# auto_sync_status:  idle
# last_synced_at:    2026-08-02T18:20:00+00:00`} />
  <p>
    Add <code>--json</code> for the full payload, which also carries recent Expo builds and
    submissions under <code>remote</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Automatic sync</h3>
  <p>
    With automatic sync on, the platform keeps the linked project and its mirrored EAS builds up
    to date without being asked. It cannot be enabled while the project is missing upstream or
    the account's token is broken — the API answers <code>422</code> telling you to reconnect:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl eas auto-sync --enabled true

# enabled:         true
# status:          idle
# error:
# last_pushed_at:  2026-08-02T18:20:00+00:00`} />

  <h3>Identifiers</h3>
  <p>
    <code>identifiers</code> compares what this platform holds against what Expo holds, per
    platform. <code>sync_status</code> is the column that matters:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>synced</code></td><td>Present on both sides</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>app_manager_only</code></td><td>Here but not on Expo; <code>sync-identifiers</code> pushes it up</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>expo_only</code></td><td>On Expo but not here; create it with <a href="/cli/identifiers"><code>appmctl identifiers create</code></a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl eas identifiers

# local_id    platform  identifier         name                    sync_status
# ----------  --------  -----------------  ----------------------  ----------------
# ident-001   ios       com.acme.customer  Customer App (iOS)      synced
# ident-002   android   com.acme.customer  Customer App (Android)  app_manager_only

appmctl eas sync-identifiers
# {"data":{"created":1,"updated":0,"unchanged":1}}`} />
  <p>
    Only iOS and Android identifiers take part; web identifiers have no meaning on Expo.
  </p>

  <h3>Credentials</h3>
  <p>
    <code>provision</code> asks Expo to produce whatever signing material the identifier is
    missing. <code>attach-credential</code> pushes one credential you already hold here up to
    Expo instead:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Let Expo generate what is missing
appmctl eas provision --identifier-id ident-001

# Restrict provisioning to specific stored credentials
appmctl eas provision --identifier-id ident-001 --credential-ids cred-001,cred-002

# Push one credential up
appmctl eas attach-credential --identifier-id ident-001 --credential-id cred-001`} />
  <p>
    These need both <code>identifiers:write</code> and <code>credentials:write</code>, since they
    move signing material between two systems.
  </p>

  <h2>The GraphQL and REST proxies</h2>
  <p>
    The platform can proxy straight to Expo's APIs. There is no wrapper for it, on purpose: the
    payloads pass through unchanged, so a wrapper would add nothing but a place for the shape to
    drift. Use <code>appmctl request</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl request POST integrations/int-002/expo/graphql \\
  --data '{"query":"{ me { username } }"}' --json

appmctl --json request GET integrations/int-002/expo/proxy/v2/projects`} />
  <p>Both need the <code>integrations:proxy</code> scope.</p>

  <h2>A first-time setup, end to end</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# 1. Connect the account
INT=$(appmctl --json eas connect --token "$EXPO_TOKEN" --account acme | jq -er '.data.id')

# 2. Link the app to an existing Expo project
appmctl --json eas projects --integration-id "$INT" |
  jq -r '.data[] | select(.exists_on_expo) | "\\(.external_id)  \\(.full_name)"'

appmctl eas link --integration-id "$INT" --mode existing \\
  --project-id 11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555

# 3. Get identifiers and credentials onto Expo
appmctl eas sync-identifiers
appmctl eas provision --identifier-id "$(appmctl --json identifiers list | jq -er '.data[0].id')"

# 4. Keep it current
appmctl eas auto-sync --enabled true
appmctl eas status`} />

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Action</th><th>Scopes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>status</code>, <code>projects</code>, <code>identifiers</code></td><td><code>integrations:read</code>, plus <code>identifiers:read</code> for identifiers</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>connect</code>, <code>sync</code>, <code>disconnect</code>, <code>auto-sync</code>, <code>link</code>, <code>import</code></td><td><code>integrations:write</code>, plus <code>apps:write</code> and <code>credentials:write</code> for the app-scoped ones</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>sync-identifiers</code></td><td><code>identifiers:write</code>, <code>integrations:write</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>provision</code>, <code>attach-credential</code></td><td><code>credentials:write</code>, <code>integrations:write</code>, and <code>identifiers:write</code> for provisioning</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Every action also requires the <code>manage_integrations</code> team permission, and the
    credential ones additionally <code>manage_credentials</code>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
